> In learning to write patches for Nethack, I've noticed that everyone
> includes the date and time in the headers. This seems very official
> and rigorous, but I've been feeling lazy and wondering what exactly
> their utility is.

Run several windows.  Use "date" in one window, cut-n-paste it
into the comment at the top using your favorite editor in another
window.  Your favorite editor is either vi or emacs, right?  ;^)

> Doesn't the OS automatically show the date of last
> modification anyway?

The OS stores the most recent modification date (along with
assorted other time stamps) in the inode or directory entry.
Modify the file more than once (another year another patch ...)
and you have lost the previous modification date.  Copy it to
another system and you might have lost the modification date.

> The "patch" man page implies that their inclusion is for the benefit
> of the --set-utc option, but what is the benefit of that? Is it for
> keeping binary distros tidy?

The date strings can be compared and the most recent version
automatically used.



